GITHUB SETUP + UPLOAD NOTES

1. Create/Open Project Folder
Example:
D:\OOPs-Java

Open this folder in IntelliJ IDEA.

--------------------------------------------------

2. Initialize Git

Command:
git init

Meaning:
Starts Git in the current project folder.

It creates a hidden folder:
.git

You only need to do this once for a project.

--------------------------------------------------

3. Connect Local Project to GitHub Repository

Command:
git remote add origin https://github.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java.git

Meaning:
Connects the project on your computer to your GitHub repository.

origin = nickname for the GitHub repository.

--------------------------------------------------

4. Check GitHub Connection

Command:
git remote -v

Meaning:
Shows which GitHub repository is connected.

Example output:

origin  https://github.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java.git (push)

--------------------------------------------------

5. Set Main Branch

Command:
git branch -M main

Meaning:
Names the main branch as "main".

This is normally done only once.

==================================================
NORMAL WORKFLOW FOR UPLOADING CODE
==================================================

1. Check Changes

Command:
git status

Meaning:
Shows:
- New files
- Modified files
- Deleted files
- Files ready to commit

--------------------------------------------------

2. Add Changes

Command:
git add .

Meaning:
Adds all changed/new files to the staging area.

"." means all files in the current project.

--------------------------------------------------

3. Check Again

Command:
git status

Meaning:
Confirms which files are ready to be committed.

--------------------------------------------------

4. Commit Changes

Command:
git commit -m "Add classes and objects"

Meaning:
Saves a version/snapshot of your code locally.

-m = message

Example messages:

git commit -m "Add constructor concept"

git commit -m "Add inheritance concept"

git commit -m "Practice polymorphism"

--------------------------------------------------

5. Push to GitHub

Command:
git push origin main

Meaning:
Uploads your committed code to GitHub.

origin = GitHub repository
main = main branch

==================================================
EASY ORDER TO REMEMBER
==================================================

git status
     ↓
git add .
     ↓
git status
     ↓
git commit -m "message"
     ↓
git push origin main

SHORT FORM:

STATUS
ADD
COMMIT
PUSH

--------------------------------------------------

IMPORTANT:

git add .
= prepares files

git commit -m "message"
= saves changes locally

git push origin main
= actually uploads changes to GitHub

--------------------------------------------------

ONE-TIME SETUP COMMANDS:

git init
git remote add origin https://github.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java.git
git branch -M main
git remote -v

--------------------------------------------------

COMMANDS USED REGULARLY:

git status
git add .
git status
git commit -m "your message"
git push origin main

now if you want to download the git folder
git clone https://github.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java.git

if you want to download the file the command is
1) for cmd
curl.exe -O "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java/main/src/classes_objects/Main.java"

2) for powershell
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java/main/src/classes_objects/Main.java" -OutFile "Main.java"

***
For curl.exe, if you want to give the downloaded file a custom name, use lowercase -o:
curl.exe -o MyFile.java "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java/main/src/classes_objects/Main.java"

Difference:
-O  = keep original filename from URL
-o  = give your own filename

For git clone, if you want a custom folder name:
git clone https://github.com/MayurThaware122/OOPS-Java.git MyFolder
